Legal systems assume individuals can choose their actions, which is the basis for assigning blame and punishment.
If free will didn't exist, moral judgments would lack a foundation, as people wouldn't be able to choose their behavior.
Without free will, justice systems would be unable to hold people accountable for their actions, undermining the purpose of punishment.
The idea of ethical behavior relies on the belief that individuals can make choices, which is only possible if they have free will.
If free will is an illusion, then the entire framework of morality and justice becomes arbitrary and meaningless.